This review covers the support knowledge-base export dated 4 October 2026, not the current
support queue. The original incident page marked the whole ticket as known with one parser
fix queued. Its table groups by tenant locale and booking form, so it cannot distinguish a
timing report from a month/day swap. This review reads the report bodies as well.

<Callout>
Do not close every report in #4471 when the parser fix ships. The form-hint problem needs a
different fix, and the timing reports need investigation before a cause can be confirmed.
</Callout>

## Reviewed report counts

<IncidentReview />

## Shared parser: month-first interpretation

The shared parser reads dates month first, but the tenant directory identifies day-first
locales. App and email reports, and booking-v2 form reports, belong to this parser problem
unless their reviewed bodies require separate timing investigation. Browser locale and intake
tags are not substitutes for the tenant's configuration.

R-41200 describes 7 April becoming 4 July. R-41205 comes from Morrow Tax, whose tenant locale
is fr-FR, even though intake saw an en-US browser and tagged it as a hint problem. The
requested fix is to parse according to the tenant's declared date order and reject ambiguous
input when its format is not known.

## booking-v3: the form tells people the wrong date order

The booking-v3 form prints MM/DD/YYYY under its date field. Alder Physio, Copper Studio and
Pale Harbour use that form with day-first tenant locales. People follow the month-first hint,
but the form reads their input in the tenant's day-first order. This is a separate interface
contract problem, not the shared parser fix.

R-41230 is a Copper Studio form report: 6 January becomes 1 June. The requested fix is to
derive the visible date hint, validation and form parsing from the same tenant format, and
test booking-v2 and booking-v3 separately.

## Timing: keep the uncertainty visible

The desk notes identify reports with adjacent-day or other timing symptoms. The tenant-only
classifier files them under parser, but a month/day swap does not explain their reported
behavior. R-41236 says an invite arrived before its booking; that observation is not enough
to prove a one-day shift.

The investigation must retain the original booking date, timezone, serialized timestamp,
recipient timezone and delivery logs. Test a single event across Lisbon and Toronto and
check date-only values around midnight and daylight-saving boundaries. These are diagnostic
checks, not a claim that a timezone bug has already been reproduced.

## Why the merge hid separate problems

Decision 0001 permits merges on normalized subject alone, without reading the body. The
accepted triage page still repeats that rule, even though its interactive example computes
cause using tenant locale and channel. Neither subject equality nor that helper alone proves
that the bodies describe the same failure.

Match tenant configuration, input path, observed date transformation and reproduction
evidence before merging. Keep unresolved timing reports separate. Do not rewrite the
accepted decision: propose a new decision that replaces subject-only merging after desk
approval.

## What the desk must check next

The support desk keeps working while this export is reviewed. Check the live queue for new
reports, changed merge assignments, shipped fixes and the current owner of each ticket.
Recompute counts from unique IDs after that check. Do not present this export's counts as
live totals, and do not mark a proposal as accepted without the desk's approval.

## Reproducing this page

The words live in this page. The tables and report IDs are computed by IncidentReview from
the unchanged report export, tenant directory and desk notes. The review helper removes
overlapping report IDs and separates the notes' timing cases before reusing the existing
cause classifier. It does not invent a timezone root cause or change the original export.

The public repository contains the source page and a prerendered HTML copy under docs.
The HTML uses these components at build time, so it includes the tables and desk reference,
not only the Markdown source.

